+## Deploying
+
+The included [Kamal configuration](config/deploy.yml) is for a single Linux server. It runs the web server and Solid Queue in one Rails container, stores the SQLite databases and uploaded files in a persistent Docker volume, and uses `kamal-proxy` for automatic HTTPS certificates. The local Docker registry and proxy containers run on that same server.
+
+Before deploying:
+
+1. Point the desired hostname at the server and allow SSH, HTTP, and HTTPS traffic to it. `kamal setup` connects as root unless you configure `ssh.user`.
+2. In `config/deploy.yml`, replace the server address, `fizzy.example.com`, and the SMTP values with your server, hostname, and mail provider details. The example assumes an authenticated STARTTLS server on port 587; also change `builder.arch` if your server is not amd64.
+3. Generate a `SECRET_KEY_BASE` with `bin/rails secret`, and provide `SMTP_USERNAME` and `SMTP_PASSWORD` from your mail provider. Generate VAPID keys with the [Web Push instructions](docs/development.md#web-push-notifications) for `VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY` and `VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY`.
+4. Save those values in a password manager and export them in your shell before running Kamal. [`.kamal/secrets`](.kamal/secrets) reads them from the environment so literal secrets stay out of Git and the image.
+
+Then deploy:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal setup
+```
+
+You can run `bin/kamal deploy` for later releases. The `fizzy_storage` volume contains every SQLite database and local Active Storage file, so replacing the application container preserves the data. Back up that volume separately from the server for disaster recovery.
+
+
